\title{The Discriminant-Gated $p+1$ Weakness and Its Invisibility from the Modulus}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-08-14}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
The Williams $p+1$ factoring method exploits primes $p$ for which $p+1$ is smooth, in the same way that Pollard's $p-1$ method exploits primes for which $p-1$ is smooth. We give a complete structural account of when the method succeeds and prove that its success condition is invisible from the composite modulus $N$.
On the positive side, we prove that success at a prime $p$ for base $P$ with discriminant $D = P^2-4$ is governed by a quadratic character: if $D$ is a non-residue modulo $p$ and $(p+1)\mid M$, then the Lucas value $V_M(P)\equiv 2 \pmod p$ and the gcd step returns $p$ exactly; if $D$ is a nonzero square, then $V_{p+1}\equiv P^2-2\not\equiv 2$ and the method has silently degenerated into Pollard's $p-1$ method. Sharply, $V_{p+1}\equiv 2 \pmod p$ holds if and only if $D$ is a non-residue or $D=0$. We classify the universally degenerate bases (exactly those with $|P|\le 2$) and count the good ones: exactly $(p-1)/2$ of the $p$ residues $P\in\Fp$ open the gate, via a trace parametrization $P=a+a^{-1}$ that is two-to-one away from $a=\pm 1$; bases whose discriminants differ by a nonzero square factor are gated by the same character, so $P=3$ ($D=5$) and $P=7$ ($D=45=5\cdot 3^2$) succeed on exactly the same primes.
On the negative side, we prove that no predicate of the statistic $N\mapsto (N \bmod 60060,\ \lceil \log_2 N\rceil)$ decides either the $+1$-divisibility of the smaller prime factor or the discriminant gate at that factor; the witness is the matched collision $359\cdot 5849 = 2{,}099{,}791$ against $397\cdot 5743 = 2{,}279{,}971$. The mechanism is a dichotomy: $N \bmod 3$ does determine the symmetric predicate ``exactly one of $p,q$ is $\equiv -1 \bmod 3$'', and $\leg{D}{N}=\leg{D}{p}\leg{D}{q}$ is the product of the two local characters, so every abelian statistic of $N$ is blind to the $S_2$-orbit of the factor pair. An empirical study of $40$ matched semiprime pairs confirms all of this quantitatively, including the exact coincidence between per-base success counts and $\leg{D}{p}=-1$ rates. The $p+1$ weakness is therefore real but strictly more hidden than the $p-1$ weakness: exploitable only by running the classical algorithm, never by inspecting $N$.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Fragile primes}

Let $N=pq$ be a semiprime with $p<q$. Two classical special-purpose factoring methods target \emph{fragile} primes. Pollard's $p-1$ method (1974) computes $\gcd(a^M-1,N)$ for $M=\operatorname{lcm}(1,\dots,B)$ and succeeds when the order of $a$ in $\Fp^\times$ divides $M$, in particular when $p-1$ is $B$-powersmooth. Williams' $p+1$ method (1982) replaces $\Fp^\times$ (order $p-1$) with the norm-one torus of $\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ (order $p+1$), realized through Lucas sequences, and succeeds when $p+1$ is $B$-powersmooth \emph{and}, as we make precise, when an additional character condition holds.

For key generation the relevant question is not whether such primes are dangerous---they are---but whether their presence can be detected from $N$. If it can, the modulus leaks a hint about its own factorization and one obtains a cheap screening test. This paper answers the question for the $p+1$ family in the negative, in three independent senses.

\subsection{Contributions}
\begin{enumerate}
\item A complete discriminant gate (Section~\ref{sec:gate}): the decisive congruence $V_{p+1}\equiv2\pmod p$ of the base-$P$ method holds if and only if $\leg{P^2-4}{p}=-1$ (or the degenerate $P^2-4\equiv0$ occurs).
\item Classification of degenerate bases (Section~\ref{sec:degen}): the useless bases are precisely $P\in\{-2,-1,0,1,2\}$.
\item Gate density (Section~\ref{sec:density}): exactly $(p-1)/2$ of the $p$ bases open the gate, and $\{3,5,7\}$ is effectively a pair of independent trials.
\item A general invisibility barrier and its instances (Section~\ref{sec:invis}).
\item The symmetry mechanism (Section~\ref{sec:why}): XOR-visibility in the residue channel, product-splitting in the character channel.
\item Experimental confirmation (Section~\ref{sec:exp}).
\end{enumerate}

\section{Setup and definitions}

\begin{definition}[Lucas $V$-sequence]
For a base $P$ in a commutative ring, define $V_0=2$, $V_1=P$ and $V_{n+2}=P V_{n+1}-V_n$. These are the Lucas sequences with parameters $(P,Q)=(P,1)$.
\end{definition}

The recurrence commutes with ring homomorphisms: $f(V_n(P))=V_n(f(P))$. In particular reduction modulo $p$ may be performed at any stage.

\begin{definition}[Discriminant]
The discriminant of the base $P$ is $D=P^2-4$, the discriminant of the companion polynomial $x^2-Px+1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Powersmoothness]
For $B\ge 1$ put $\lcmle:=\operatorname{lcm}(1,2,\dots,B)$. An integer $n\ge1$ is \emph{$B$-powersmooth} if $\ell^{v_\ell(n)}\le B$ for every prime $\ell$, where $v_\ell$ is the $\ell$-adic valuation.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Admissible instance]
A pair $(p,q)$ is \emph{admissible} if $p,q$ are prime and $p<q$; then ``the smaller factor'' of $N=pq$ is well defined.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Locator]\label{def:locator}
Let $\stat:\mathbb{N}\to\alpha$ be a function of the modulus and $S$ a predicate on primes. A \emph{locator for $S$ from $\stat$} is a predicate $f$ on $\alpha$ with $f(\stat(pq))\iff S(p)$ for all admissible $(p,q)$. We say $S$ is \emph{invisible from} $\stat$ when no locator exists.
\end{definition}

Definition~\ref{def:locator} is deliberately generous: $f$ may be arbitrary, non-computable, and tailored to the instance class. Impossibility at this level is therefore very strong.

\section{The discriminant gate}\label{sec:gate}

\begin{lemma}[Binet form]\label{lem:binet}
If $ab=1$ and $a+b=P$ in a commutative ring, then $V_n(P)=a^n+b^n$ for all $n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two-step induction, using $a^{n+2}+b^{n+2}=(a+b)(a^{n+1}+b^{n+1})-(ab)(a^n+b^n)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact success criterion]\label{thm:criterion}
Let $K$ be an integral domain and $a,b\in K$ with $ab=1$, $a+b=P$. Then $V_M(P)=2$ if and only if $a^M=1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
From $a^Mb^M=1$ one obtains $a^M(V_M(P)-2)=(a^M-1)^2$. If $V_M=2$ the right side vanishes, so $a^M=1$ in the domain $K$; conversely $a^M=1$ forces $b^M=1$ and $V_M=2$.
\end{proof}

Thus the Williams criterion is an order condition on a norm-one element; the divisibility $(p+1)\mid M$ is merely the hypothesis the algorithm can arrange in advance.

\begin{theorem}[$p+1$ gate, non-residue case]\label{thm:gateplus}
Let $p$ be an odd prime and $P\in\Fp$ with $D=P^2-4$ a quadratic non-residue mod $p$. Then $V_M(P)\equiv 2 \pmod p$ for every $M$ with $(p+1)\mid M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Since $D$ is a non-residue, $K=\Fp[x]/(x^2-D)\cong\mathbb{F}_{p^2}$ is a field of characteristic $p$ containing a square root $s$ of $D$. Euler's criterion gives $D^{(p-1)/2}=-1$, hence $s^p=(s^2)^{(p-1)/2}s=-s$. Put $a=(P+s)/2$, $b=(P-s)/2$; then $a+b=P$ and $ab=(P^2-s^2)/4=1$. Frobenius is additive in characteristic $p$, so $a^p=b$ and $b^p=a$, whence $a^{p+1}=ab=1$ and likewise for $b$. For $M=(p+1)k$ we get $a^M=b^M=1$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:binet} gives $V_M=2$ in $K$; injectivity of $\Fp\hookrightarrow K$ transfers the congruence.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Integer form]\label{cor:int}
If $p$ is an odd prime, $P\in\mathbb{Z}$, $\leg{P^2-4}{p}=-1$ and $(p+1)\mid M$, then $p\mid V_M(P)-2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}[Split roots]\label{lem:split}
If $t^2\equiv P^2-4 \pmod p$ with $p$ odd, then $a=(P+t)/2$ and $b=(P-t)/2$ lie in $\Fp$ with $ab=1$ and $a+b=P$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Degeneration to Pollard]\label{thm:gateminus}
If $D=P^2-4$ is a square modulo the odd prime $p$, then $V_M(P)\equiv 2\pmod p$ whenever $(p-1)\mid M$, and
\[ V_{p+1}(P)\equiv P^2-2 \pmod p. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Lemma~\ref{lem:split} puts $a,b\in\Fp^\times$; Fermat gives $a^{p-1}=b^{p-1}=1$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:criterion} applies. For the value, $a^p=a$, $b^p=b$, so $V_{p+1}=a^2+b^2=(a+b)^2-2ab=P^2-2$.
\end{proof}

When the gate is closed the method is not merely slow: the relevant group is $\Fp^\times$ of order $p-1$, so smoothness of $p+1$ is irrelevant.

\begin{theorem}[Sharp gate]\label{thm:sharp}
For an odd prime $p$ and any $P\in\Fp$,
\[ V_{p+1}(P)=2 \iff \bigl(P^2-4 \text{ is a non-residue}\bigr)\ \text{or}\ \bigl(P^2-4=0\bigr). \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:gateplus} with $M=p+1$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:gateminus} with $t=0$ (then $P^2-2=2$). ($\Rightarrow$) If $D$ is a nonzero square, $V_{p+1}=P^2-2$, and $P^2-2=2$ would force $D=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exactness of the gcd step]\label{thm:gcd}
If $p,q$ are primes, $p\mid V$ and $q\nmid V$, then $\gcd(V,pq)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Positive control, capstone]\label{thm:capstone}
Let $p$ be an odd prime, $q$ prime, $P\in\mathbb{Z}$ with $\leg{P^2-4}{p}=-1$, and suppose $p+1$ is $B$-powersmooth. If $q\nmid V_M(P)-2$ for $M=\lcmle$, then $\gcd(V_M(P)-2,\ pq)=p$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Powersmoothness gives $(p+1)\mid \lcmle$, since $v_\ell(p+1)\le v_\ell(\lcmle)$ for every prime $\ell$ (because $\ell^{v_\ell(p+1)}\in\{1,\dots,B\}$). Apply Corollary~\ref{cor:int} and Theorem~\ref{thm:gcd}.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}[Tropical shape of smoothness]
The hypothesis ``$\ell^{v_\ell(n)}\le B$ for all $\ell$'' bounds the \emph{maximum} of the quantities $v_\ell(n)\log\ell$: a sup-norm constraint on the valuation vector, i.e.\ a max-plus (tropical) condition rather than a multiplicative one. The divisibility $n\mid\lcmle$ says exactly that the valuation vector lies in the resulting tropical box. The same flavour recurs in Section~\ref{sec:why}, where the returned factor is the divisor on the lower side of the corner of a piecewise-linear function.
\end{remark}

\paragraph{Worked instance.} For $N=91=7\cdot 13$, $P=3$ ($D=5$), $M=8$: $5$ is a non-residue mod $7$ and $8=7+1$, so $7\mid V_8-2$; indeed $V_8(3)=2207$ and $\gcd(2205,91)=7$. At $p=11$ the discriminant $5$ \emph{is} a square ($4^2\equiv 5$), and $V_{12}(3)\equiv 3^2-2=7\not\equiv 2 \pmod{11}$: the gate is genuinely necessary.

\section{Which bases are usable?}\label{sec:degen}

\begin{definition}
A base $P\in\mathbb{Z}$ is \emph{universally degenerate} if $V_k(P)=2$ for some $k\ge1$ as an identity over $\mathbb{Z}$.
\end{definition}

For such a base $\gcd(V_M-2,N)=N$ for every $N$ and every $M$ divisible by $k$: no factor is ever produced.

\begin{lemma}[Periodicity]
If $V_k(P)=2$ and $V_{k+1}(P)=P$, then $V_{n+k}(P)=V_n(P)$ for all $n$, hence $V_M(P)=2$ for every multiple $M$ of $k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}[The five bad bases]
The bases $2,-2,-1,0,1$ are universally degenerate with periods $1,2,3,4,6$. These are exactly the $P=2\cos\theta$ for which the root $a=e^{i\theta}$ is a root of unity of order $1,2,3,4$ or $6$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{lemma}[Growth]
For integer $P\ge3$, $V_{n+1}(P)\ge3$ and $V_{n+1}(P)<V_{n+2}(P)$ for all $n\ge0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Classification of degenerate bases]
For $P\in\mathbb{Z}$: there exists $k\ge1$ with $V_k(P)=2$ if and only if $|P|\le2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $P\ge3$ the growth lemma gives $V_k\ge3$ for all $k\ge1$; the sign symmetry $V_n(-P)=(-1)^nV_n(P)$ transfers the statement to $P\le-3$. The converse is the previous proposition.
\end{proof}

This justifies the standard implementation choice of starting the base search at $P=3$.

\section{How many bases open the gate?}\label{sec:density}

\begin{theorem}[Gate density]\label{thm:density}
For an odd prime $p$,
\[ \#\{P\in\Fp: P^2-4 \text{ is a square}\}=\frac{p+1}{2},\qquad \#\{P\in\Fp: P^2-4 \text{ is a non-residue}\}=\frac{p-1}{2}. \]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Consider the trace map $g:\Fp^\times\to\Fp$, $g(a)=a+a^{-1}$. If $P=g(a)$ then $P^2-4=(a-a^{-1})^2$ is a square. Conversely if $P^2-4=t^2$ then by Lemma~\ref{lem:split} the roots $a,b=(P\pm t)/2$ lie in $\Fp^\times$ with $ab=1$, so $P=a+a^{-1}$. Hence the bad bases form exactly $g(\Fp^\times)$. Since $g(a)=g(a')$ iff $a'\in\{a,a^{-1}\}$, the map is two-to-one except at the fixed points $a=\pm1$, so $\#g(\Fp^\times)=\frac{(p-1)-2}{2}+2=\frac{p+1}{2}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A uniformly random base opens the gate with probability $(p-1)/(2p)\to 1/2$. At $p=11$: six bad bases, five good ones.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Square-class invariance]\label{thm:sqclass}
If $c\not\equiv0\pmod p$ then $\leg{Dc^2}{p}=\leg{D}{p}$. In particular $\leg{45}{p}=\leg{5}{p}$ for $p\neq3$, so the bases $P=3$ ($D=5$) and $P=7$ ($D=45=5\cdot3^2$) are gated by the same character and succeed at exactly the same primes.
\end{theorem}

Consequently a base list should have pairwise distinct discriminant square classes: trying $\{3,5,7\}$ costs three Lucas exponentiations but delivers only two independent gate trials, so the heuristic success probability against a random prime is about $3/4$, not $7/8$.

\section{Invisibility from the modulus}\label{sec:invis}

\begin{theorem}[No locator from a collision]\label{thm:barrier}
Let $\stat$ be any function of the modulus and $S$ any predicate on primes. If there are admissible pairs $(p,q)$ and $(p',q')$ with $\stat(pq)=\stat(p'q')$, $S(p)$ true and $S(p')$ false, then $S$ is invisible from $\stat$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f$ were a locator, then $f(\stat(pq))$ holds since $S(p)$ holds; rewriting along the collision gives $f(\stat(p'q'))$, hence $S(p')$ --- a contradiction.
\end{proof}

The statement is quantified over \emph{all} predicates $f$, so no amount of computation or model fitting evades it: one collision closes an entire attack surface.

Let
\[ \stat(N)=\bigl(N \bmod 60060,\ \operatorname{bitlength}(N)\bigr),\qquad 60060=2^2\cdot3\cdot5\cdot7\cdot11\cdot13 . \]
This determines $N \bmod \ell$ for every prime $\ell\le 13$, every Jacobi symbol $\leg{d}{N}$ with $d$ supported on $\{-1,2,3,5,7,11,13\}$ up to squares, and the size information used to match instances.

\begin{lemma}[Matched collision]\label{lem:collision}
With $N=359\cdot5849=2{,}099{,}791$ and $N'=397\cdot5743=2{,}279{,}971$:
\[ N\equiv N'\equiv 57{,}751 \pmod{60060},\qquad \operatorname{bitlength}(N)=\operatorname{bitlength}(N')=22, \]
and the small factors share a bit length ($9$), as do the large factors ($13$).
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Opposite labels]\label{lem:labels}
$3\mid 359+1$ but $3\nmid 397+1$; and $\leg{5}{397}=-1$ but $\leg{5}{359}=+1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$360=3\cdot120$ and $398=3\cdot132+2$. Since $5\equiv1\pmod4$, reciprocity gives $\leg{5}{p}=\leg{p}{5}$; as $359\equiv4$ and $397\equiv2\pmod 5$, and the squares mod $5$ are $\{1,4\}$, the values are $+1$ and $-1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Invisibility of $+1$-divisibility]\label{thm:invis1}
There is no predicate $f$ with $f\bigl(pq \bmod 60060,\ \operatorname{bitlength}(pq)\bigr)\iff 3\mid p+1$ for all admissible $(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Invisibility of the discriminant gate]\label{thm:invis2}
There is no predicate $f$ with $f\bigl(pq \bmod 60060,\ \operatorname{bitlength}(pq)\bigr)\iff \leg{5}{p}=-1$ for all admissible $(p,q)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Both follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} using Lemmas~\ref{lem:collision} and~\ref{lem:labels}, in the two respective orders. Note that the same collision serves both channels, with the labels swapped.
\end{proof}

Since $\leg{5}{p}=-1$ says exactly that $D=3^2-4=5$ is a non-residue mod $p$, Theorem~\ref{thm:invis2} asserts the invisibility of the very condition that Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} identifies as equivalent to success of the base-$3$ method.

\begin{corollary}[Per-modulus corollaries]
For every $\ell\mid 60060$---in particular $\ell=3,5,7,11,13$---neither $3\mid p+1$ nor $\leg{5}{p}=-1$ is decidable from $N \bmod \ell$, since a locator from $N \bmod \ell$ composes with reduction to give one from the richer statistic.
\end{corollary}

\section{Why: the symmetry mechanism}\label{sec:why}

\subsection{The residue channel: XOR is visible, the label is not}

\begin{theorem}[$+1$-divisibility dichotomy]\label{thm:xor}
Let $p,q$ be primes different from $3$ and $N=pq$. Then $N\equiv2\pmod3$ if and only if exactly one of $3\mid p+1$, $3\mid q+1$ holds.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Neither factor is divisible by $3$, so each residue is $1$ or $2$, and $3\mid p+1$ means $p\equiv2$. Multiplication mod $3$ on $\{1,2\}$ is the group $\mathbb{Z}/2$: the product is $2$ iff exactly one factor is $2$.
\end{proof}

Thus a single cheap residue test resolves the XOR of the two local bits, while the ``which factor'' question survives untouched. XOR is invariant under swapping $p$ and $q$; the label is not.

\subsection{The character channel: products destroy the split}

\begin{theorem}[Jacobi splitting]\label{thm:jacobi}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and any $D\in\mathbb{Z}$, $\leg{D}{pq}=\leg{D}{p}\leg{D}{q}$, the left side being the Jacobi symbol.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Product uninformativeness]
$\leg{5}{21}=+1$ with $\leg{5}{3}=-1$ (gate open at the smaller factor), while $\leg{5}{209}=+1$ with $\leg{5}{11}=+1$ (gate closed). Hence no predicate of $\leg{5}{N}$ decides the gate at the smaller factor.
\end{proposition}

This is the sense in which the $p+1$ weakness is strictly more hidden than the $p-1$ weakness: the exploitable condition \emph{is} a quadratic character of the hidden factor, and the only corresponding character of $N$ is the product of the two hidden ones. Every abelian invariant of $N$ --- residues, Jacobi symbols, Dirichlet characters, Artin symbols in abelian extensions --- is such a product, hence invariant under the swap.

\subsection{The tropical corner}

The divisor pairs of $N$ lie on $xy=N$, whose logarithmic picture has a corner at $x=y=\sqrt N$; for any divisor $d$, $\min(d,N/d)\le\sqrt N\le\max(d,N/d)$.

\begin{proposition}[Output localization]
When the method succeeds at the smaller factor of $N=pq$, the value returned is $p$ itself, and $p\le\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor$.
\end{proposition}

The algorithm thus locates a lattice point in the window $[1,\sqrt N]$ that, by Theorems~\ref{thm:invis1} and~\ref{thm:invis2}, no statistic of $N$ can point to.

\section{Experimental protocol and measurements}\label{sec:exp}

\subsection{Design}
Forty matched pairs of semiprimes were generated. Within each pair the smaller factor had bit length $18$ and the larger $21$; the two instances differed only in whether the smaller factor's $p+1$ was smooth. The PLUSONE class has $p+1\mid M$ with $M=\operatorname{lcm}(1,\dots,100)$; the GENERAL class has no smoothness engineered on $p\pm1$ or $q\pm1$. Matching bit lengths removes size as a confounder.

\subsection{Positive control}
