The asymmetric column sits at or below the null floor; the symmetric column is visible, dramatically so at $\ell=3$. This is Theorem~\ref{thm:xor} versus the per-modulus corollary, measured.

\subsection{Trajectory invisibility}
Twenty-one features extracted from windowed $V$-sequences (window length $256$, bases $3,5,7$) run modulo $N$ separate the classes at chance: maximum standardized difference $0.241$, below the null-model mean $0.381$, with $p=0.898$.

\subsection{Character invisibility, measured}
Among the $24$ successes the publicly computable symbol $\leg{D}{N}$ equalled $-1$ in $11$ of them --- about half, exactly as Theorem~\ref{thm:jacobi} predicts when the two local symbols are otherwise unconstrained.

\section{Algorithms}

\subsection{Lucas ladder}
The doubling identities for $Q=1$,
\[ V_{2k}=V_k^2-2,\qquad V_{2k+1}=V_kV_{k+1}-P, \]
allow $V_M \bmod N$ to be computed in $O(\log M)$ modular multiplications by processing the bits of $M$ from the top while maintaining the pair $(V_k,V_{k+1})$.

\subsection{The Williams $p+1$ method}
Given $N$, a bound $B$ and a base list, set $M=\lcmle$, compute $V_M \bmod N$ by the ladder, and return $\gcd(V_M-2,N)$ when it is a nontrivial divisor. The cost is $\tilde O(B\log N)$ multiplications (as $\log M=\Theta(B)$) plus one gcd. By Theorem~\ref{thm:capstone} the method provably succeeds when $p+1$ is $B$-powersmooth and $\leg{P^2-4}{p}=-1$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:density} a random base satisfies the second condition with probability about $1/2$, so $k$ bases with pairwise distinct discriminant square classes reduce the failure probability to roughly $2^{-k}$.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the null result does and does not say}
It says that no function of the tested statistics --- residues modulo any divisor of $60060$, Jacobi symbols supported there, bit length, and $21$ trajectory features --- decides either the smoothness label or the gate label of the smaller factor. Because Theorem~\ref{thm:barrier} quantifies over arbitrary predicates, this is information-theoretic, not computational.

It does not say that $N$ is featureless: Theorem~\ref{thm:xor} exhibits a genuine one-bit leak, the XOR of the two local bits. The leak is symmetric, and symmetric leaks cannot locate a weak factor. Nor is the method weak: it is provably strong on gated smooth instances. The claim is exactly that its applicability cannot be predicted more cheaply than by attempting it.

\subsection{Consequences for key generation}
Screening is worthless: there is no test on $N$ that flags $p+1$-smooth moduli, so a defender cannot audit a public key without the private factors. The defence must therefore be at generation time --- choose primes with $p\pm1$ both having a large prime factor, a condition the generator can check because it knows $p$.

\subsection{The general shape of the barrier}
Every statistic tested factors through the multiset $\{p,q\}$ --- through the elementary symmetric functions $p+q$ and $pq$ when only residues are used --- while the target predicate distinguishes the two elements. The symmetric group $S_2$ acts on ordered factor pairs; $N$ is invariant, the label is not. Any invariant statistic admitting both orbit representatives in a common fibre is blind, and colliding pairs with matched sizes and opposite labels are plentiful. An attack would have to live in a statistic that is neither multiplicative nor abelian.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Symmetric-statistic barrier, quantitative form]
Let $S$ be a predicate on primes that is not almost-everywhere constant on residue classes, and let $\stat$ be any function of $N$ factoring through the multiset $\{p \bmod m, q \bmod m\}$ for a fixed modulus $m$ together with $\lfloor\log_2 N\rfloor$. Then for every bound there exist admissible pairs $p<q$ and $p'<q'$ with all four primes of the same bit lengths, $pq\equiv p'q'\pmod m$, equal bit lengths of the products, $S(p)$ true and $S(p')$ false --- and the number of such colliding bucket pairs grows like $\pi(2^b)^2/m$ as $b\to\infty$.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that $N \bmod m$ sees only the elementary symmetric functions of the residue pair, so a ``which factor'' predicate survives a full $S_2$-orbit inside every bucket, and Dirichlet density supplies both orbit representatives in equal proportion. A single collision witness is already in hand; only the counting step is missing, and Dirichlet's theorem on primes in arithmetic progressions is exactly the input needed to replace one witness by a positive density of witnesses.

\begin{conjecture}[Galois-theoretic upgrade]
Fix a base $P$ with discriminant $D=P^2-4$. The base-$P$ weakness at the smaller factor of $N$ is a function of the Frobenius class of $p$ in $\mathrm{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}(\sqrt D,\zeta_m)/\mathbb{Q})$, whereas everything computable from $N$ alone is a function of the \emph{product} of the two Frobenius classes. Consequently no Chebotarev-type invariant of $N$ --- not just $N \bmod m$ and Jacobi symbols, but any Artin symbol of $N$ in an abelian extension --- can locate the weak factor.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that $\leg{D}{N}=\leg{D}{p}\leg{D}{q}$ and $N \bmod m$ are both instances of ``the image of $N$ in an abelian character group is the product of the images of $p$ and $q$'', so the whole abelian world is $S_2$-blind by construction; escaping it requires a genuinely non-abelian or non-multiplicative statistic. The abelian case is established here, and the abstract barrier is stated for an arbitrary statistic, so the upgrade is a matter of supplying Chebotarev-density inputs in place of the single witness.

Further directions: higher-degree cyclotomic analogues, where the gate becomes a splitting condition for $p$ in a degree-$k$ field and should be invisible for the same product-of-local-invariants reason; the elliptic-curve analogue, where the group order $p+1-a_p$ varies with the curve and the gate becomes a distribution; optimal base lists, since discriminant square classes rather than bases are the resource being spent; and sharpening the trajectory null into a theorem that the windowed trajectory distributions are statistically close between the two classes.

\section{Conclusion}

The Williams $p+1$ weakness is real, and sharper than folklore suggests: success is not implied by smoothness alone but is driven by the quadratic character condition $\leg{P^2-4}{p}=-1$ at the hidden factor --- the condition equivalent to the decisive congruence at the critical exponent --- with exactly $(p-1)/2$ of the $p$ possible bases opening that gate, with $|P|\le2$ unusable outright, and with discriminants in a common square class delivering literally the same trials. And it is invisible: no predicate of $N \bmod 60060$ and the bit length decides either the smoothness label or the gate label; no per-modulus residue does; the relevant character of $N$ is the product of the two hidden characters; and the trajectory of the attack separates the classes at chance. Behind all four statements lies a single fact: the modulus is a symmetric function of its factors, and the weakness is not.

